aria2 for OpenHarmony
=====================

This tree cross-compiles shared ``libaria2.so`` (including ABI v1 C lifecycle
entry points) and the compatible ``aria2c`` command-line executable for:

* ``arm64-v8a`` (``aarch64-unknown-linux-ohos``)
* ``armeabi-v7a`` (``armv7-unknown-linux-ohos``)
* ``x86_64`` (``x86_64-unknown-linux-ohos``)

The build enables the same main features as the Android build: HTTP/HTTPS,
FTP, BitTorrent, Metalink, XML-RPC, asynchronous DNS, SFTP, GZip and
WebSocket. SQLite and NLS are disabled.

Requirements
------------

* OpenHarmony native SDK (``OHOS_NDK_HOME``)
* Autotools: autoconf, automake, libtool, gettext, pkg-config
* PIC static OpenSSL 1.1, expat, zlib, c-ares and libssh2 libraries installed
  under one prefix, including notices and ``dependencies.json``.
* curl, jq, Perl/shasum, tar and zip for reproducible staging/packaging.

Like ``android-config``, ``ohos-config`` only configures aria2 and assumes its
dependencies have already been built. The OpenHarmony SDK itself only
provides zlib from this list. ``ohos/build-deps`` builds the baseline dependency
versions as PIC without modifying the OpenSSL checkout. It pins the OpenSSL
1.1 producer revision ``0f7849a999d3c69f8d2ed23bf5f8d680a2aac6be`` and validates
the first-download SHA-256 pins in ``ohos/dependencies.sha256``. Those pins are
not independently authenticated vendor digests. The baseline versions have not
passed security/release review; this task does not upgrade them.

The pinned producer's actual ``opensslv.h`` reports
``OpenSSL 1.1.1x-dev  xx XXX xxxx`` and ``OPENSSL_VERSION_NUMBER=0x10101180L``;
it is not the released 1.1.1w. Both native and Docker metadata derive
``version_header`` from that header rather than assigning a release label.
Staging validates the pinned header and exact upstream-license bytes, records
their hashes, and uses revision-neutral ``OpenSSL-LICENSE.txt``. A retained
prefix with the historical 1.1.1w notice filename is read only: only its new
staged copy is normalized. No dependency source, binaries or ABI are changed.

Build
-----

::

    export OHOS_NDK_HOME=/path/to/openharmony
    export OHOS_OPENSSL_REPO=/path/to/read-only/ohos-openssl
    export OHOS_DEPS_PREFIX=/path/to/fresh/ohos-dependencies/arm64-v8a
    LIBTOOLIZE=glibtoolize autoreconf -fi   # macOS; use libtoolize on Linux
    ./ohos/build-deps
    ./ohos-config aarch64

Repeat with ``armv7`` or ``x86_64`` when those ABIs are needed. Artifacts
are written to ``ohos-out/<abi>``. Fresh build directories are retained under
``ohos-build/<abi>/run.*``; no existing directory is recursively deleted.
Existing outputs/prefixes are rejected. To rebuild, choose fresh
``OHOS_OUT_DIR`` and ``OHOS_DEPS_PREFIX`` (or reuse an already validated prefix
without rerunning ``build-deps``). ``OHOS_BUILD_DIR`` may explicitly select a
fresh build directory.

Library sources and all static inputs use PIC; PIE applies only to CLI main/link.
The CLI statically embeds the engine, preserving its lack of a libaria2 runtime
dependency; both deliverables require the packaged C++ runtime.
The OHOS shared target uses the unversioned SONAME ``libaria2.so`` so the final
HAP can carry that exact filename. The compiler's ordinary ``libaria2.so``
output is staged directly as the ordinary file ``lib/libaria2.so``. Delivery
and packages forbid symlinks, versioned library files and a top-level
convenience alias; no unpack-time dereferencing is needed. HAP packagers must
copy this file to their ABI library path and package the observed
``libc++_shared.so`` runtime as well; final HAP/device loading is still a
separate, unperformed acceptance gate. ``libc.so`` is supplied by OHOS.

Package one built ABI with::

    ./ohos-release aarch64

Both ZIP and tar.gz contain the same ordinary-file native layout. Symlink or
versioned-library inputs are rejected before packaging. Packages, extracted staging
directories and package hashes are retained under ``ohos-packages``. Set a fresh
``BUILD_VER`` or ``OHOS_PACKAGE_DIR`` for another package. They contain headers,
native runtimes, third-party notices, SHA256SUMS, ``artifact-manifest.json``, ELF
reports, base revision/dirty diff and a build-source snapshot. The post-build
implementation evidence report is excluded from the source hash/snapshot to
avoid self-referential final-hash reporting.
The OpenSSL prebuilt producer's upstream source/patch offer must still be
completed before redistribution; included MIT producer notices are not a
substitute for the OpenSSL 1.1 license. No G0/security/release PASS is implied.

Run the native-file layout regression test against a built output::

    ./ohos/artifact-layout-test /absolute/path/to/ohos-out/arm64-v8a \
      "$OHOS_NDK_HOME" aarch64-unknown-linux-ohos arm64-v8a

This verifies ordinary-file layout and that manifest/package generation rejects
library or unrelated symlinks, versioned library files and top-level aliases.
It retains invalid test copies in isolated temporary directories and never
rewrites the input artifact.

Docker
------

``Dockerfile.ohos`` builds PIC arm64 dependencies, the shared library, CLI and
release packages using the same scripts.
It requires a Linux x86_64 OpenHarmony native SDK and the local
``ohos-openssl`` repository as BuildKit contexts. The OpenSSL context must
include its Git metadata and the pinned OpenSSL producer commit above::

    docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64 \
      --build-context ohos-sdk=/path/to/linux/openharmony \
      --build-context ohos-openssl=/path/to/ohos-openssl \
      --output type=local,dest=ohos-docker-out \
      -f Dockerfile.ohos .

The release packages are written to ``ohos-docker-out``. A macOS DevEco LLVM
toolchain is Mach-O and cannot be used in this Linux container: use a Linux SDK
context, or cross-build directly on macOS using its native SDK. This task's
actual artifact uses the macOS SDK; the Docker path is not independently run.
Docker excludes .git and generated builds/downloads. Its manifest records the
declared ARIA2_SOURCE_BASE_REVISION separately from the full context hash; it
does not pretend an uncommitted worktree context is a clean immutable commit.

C ABI lifecycle
---------------

Include ``<aria2/aria2_c.h>``; compile the consumer as C or call the five
unmangled exported symbols from Rust. No C++ or Rust allocator type crosses
this local boundary. It is NOT a child-process entry, IPC layer or process API.

1. ``aria2_c_initialize(options, count, &owner)`` initializes global dependencies
   and one keep-running session with signal handling disabled.
2. Repeatedly call ``aria2_c_run_once(owner, &running)`` on that same owner
   thread. This uses upstream RUN_ONCE: its idle poll is up to about one second,
   and internal no-wait draining/file I/O means there is no hard wall-clock
   deadline. A Rust child must keep its control-channel listener separate and
   deliver shutdown intent to the owner between iterations, never call Session
   APIs from another thread.
3. Request graceful shutdown with ``aria2_c_shutdown(owner, 0)``. Continue
   iterating until running is zero. The owner may escalate to ``force=1`` after
   its caller-owned deadline; a stuck native call still requires external
   child termination. Shutdown does not join or finalize automatically.
4. ``aria2_c_finalize(&owner, &engine_result)`` releases the session, global
   library and handle, clearing owner. It rejects a live session. After an
   ENGINE_ERROR it permits cleanup but does not promise a flushed session.

Always check status before reading output values. Engine result is separate
from the C ABI status. Exceptions are caught at every fallible exported
boundary; fatal OS signals, invalid caller memory and noexcept-destructor aborts
are not recoverable exceptions. No diagnostic string is returned, preventing
accidental logging of private options.

One global lifecycle per process only; mixing with aria2c or the C++ API in the
same process is forbidden. Input prevalidation errors have not consumed global
initialization and may be corrected/retried. Once libraryInit is attempted,
initialization failure or successful finalize requires a NEW process for any
restart. initialize then returns ARIA2_C_PROCESS_RESTART_REQUIRED without
accessing the freed upstream preference globals. INIT_FAILED means initialization
consumed the lifecycle; post-init exceptions also report PROCESS_RESTART_REQUIRED.
Do not retry a bind failure by reinitializing in the same process. All lifecycle
operations must run on the creating thread.
The facade rejects concurrent calls (BUSY) and other threads (WRONG_THREAD);
its mutex is not a promise that upstream Session calls are thread-safe. Handles
cannot be copied as ownership and are invalid after finalize. The caller must
provide valid readable NUL-terminated strings and writable output pointers;
the library cannot validate arbitrary memory addresses. Strings are copied
synchronously; caller memory can be released after initialize returns.
Limits: 256 distinct options, 128-byte names, 65,536-byte values, 1 MiB aggregate.
Unknown/invalid options fail without echoing their values. Global console sinks
discard output; no config file is read and no file/console logger is enabled.

RPC requires ``enable-rpc=true``, caller-selected ``rpc-listen-port`` in
1024..65535 and ``rpc-secret`` of 32..1024 printable, non-whitespace bytes.
The caller owns entropy, collision detection and retry/port discovery; port 0
is not supported. Initialize is not a Ready signal: authenticated loopback
health probing remains the caller's responsibility. The facade always forces
``rpc-listen-all=false`` and rejects a requested public listener, config/log
override or obsolete username/password. Secrets travel only through in-memory
options, never argv/config/log. The upstream C++ option/session strings are
ordinary allocations, so secure heap zeroization is NOT guaranteed. Caller
buffers should use their own zeroizing storage. Session-save files may contain
download URLs; credential-bearing URL recovery needs a separately accepted
encrypted-source contract and is not solved by this lifecycle ABI.

Owner tests::

    ./ohos/host-c-api-test

This compiles ``test/aria2_c_lifecycle.c`` with ``cc -std=c11`` (not C++), links
to the real shared library, checks validation, 100 independent process lifecycles, single
instance/thread/signal rules and unauthenticated/authenticated loopback RPC.
An independent process checks session-initialization failure and subsequent
restart-required rejection; pure validation failures permit corrected input.
It also checks stable same-process reinitialization rejection after finalize;
it does not claim upstream global reentrancy. Host builds disable TLS only for these lifecycle tests; the actual OHOS artifact
enables the baseline OpenSSL 1.1 feature set.

API compatibility
-----------------

The intended consumer minimum is API22 and target SDK is API26. The available
SDK contains a single API26 sysroot without an independently verified API22
symbol baseline. Therefore the generated manifest explicitly records requested
minimum 22, validated minimum null and API22 status MISSING. Successful SDK26
linking, or later Phone API26 smoke, does not prove API22. Final API22 undefined
symbol audit and target-device shared loading/lifecycle/RPC/storage/soak remain
independent producer/consumer acceptance work. Do not advertise release
compatibility until those pass.

Run
---

The CLI requires its matching C++ runtime. An example
manual smoke command (not executed by this implementation task)::

    hdc file send ohos-out/arm64-v8a/aria2c /data/local/tmp/aria2c
    hdc file send ohos-out/arm64-v8a/lib/libaria2.so /data/local/tmp/libaria2.so
    hdc file send ohos-out/arm64-v8a/lib/libc++_shared.so /data/local/tmp/libc++_shared.so
    hdc shell chmod 755 /data/local/tmp/aria2c
    hdc shell 'LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/data/local/tmp /data/local/tmp/aria2c -v'

Notes
-----

* HTTPS verification needs a CA bundle. Pass it with
  ``--ca-certificate=FILE`` when the system has no default bundle.
* c-ares is built in, while asynchronous DNS defaults to off as it does on
  Android. Enable it explicitly with ``--async-dns=true`` when required.
